# Lecture 09E

## Crossovers and Tetrad Mapping

## Chapter 8

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 3, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish crossover histories from observed tetrad classes.
- Use [PD]{color=#935D00} and [NPD]{color=#007A00} counts to recognize linkage.
- Explain the different weights in recombination frequency and map distance.

=== layout: definitions

# Unordered Tetrad Classes

@body

- [*Parental Ditype (PD)*]{color=#935D00}: four spores with only the two parental allele combinations.
- [*Nonparental Ditype (NPD)*]{color=#007A00}: four spores with only the two nonparental allele combinations.
- [*Tetratype (TT)*]{color=#A719DB}: one spore of each of the four allele combinations.

=== layout: definitions

# Crossover Histories

@body

- [*NCO*]{color=#24578F}: a non-crossover meiosis in the interval being studied.
- [*SCO*]{color=#24578F}: a single-crossover meiosis in that interval.
- [*DCO*]{color=#24578F}: a double-crossover meiosis in that interval.

=== layout: section

# Tetrad Classes

=== layout: one-panel

# Two Different Kinds of Labels

@body

| Labels | What Do They Describe? |
| --- | --- |
| NCO, SCO, DCO | How many crossovers occurred in a meiosis |
| [PD]{color=#935D00}, [NPD]{color=#007A00}, [TT]{color=#A719DB} | The allele combinations observed in its four spores |

A tetrad class does not always tell us its crossover history.

